</style>

<StoryLayout story={story}>
  <div class="container">

<section class="section" id="charter">
  <h2><span class="num">01 — The Charter</span>The Document Everything Is Measured Against</h2>
  <p>On April 28, 2026, Elon Musk took the witness stand in Courtroom 1 of the Ronald V. Dellums Federal Building in Oakland and told a nine-person advisory jury that if it is acceptable to "loot a charity," then "the entire foundation of charitable giving in America will be destroyed." It was the first time he had said the words under oath. He had said versions of them on social media, in his complaint, to biographers, on podcasts. In the courtroom, with no cameras and an audio feed piped to the press, the words were on the record.</p>
  <p>Musk's testimony returned, again and again, to a single document. On December 11, 2015, OpenAI had announced itself in a blog post on its own site. The post named the founders — Sam Altman, Greg Brockman, Ilya Sutskever, and others, with Altman and Musk as co-chairs — and described a 501(c)(3) nonprofit research organization. Backers including Musk, Altman, Reid Hoffman, Peter Thiel, Jessica Livingston, Amazon Web Services, and Infosys had pledged $1 billion. The post stated the organization's goal was "to advance digital intelligence in the way that is most likely to benefit humanity as a whole, unconstrained by a need to generate financial return." That sentence is the one Musk's complaint cites first. It is the sentence everything that followed is being measured against.</p>
  <p>How much Musk himself put in is contested. OpenAI's filings cite $38 million; Musk's filings and several court documents cite approximately $44 million; some outlets carry $45 million. The trial may settle this. Musk also testified to the personal motivation: that in 2015, Google co-founder Larry Page had dismissed his concerns about AI risk and called him a "speciesist," and that this exchange convinced him a counterweight to Google's AI program was needed in nonprofit form. Page has not publicly responded. A version of the exchange appears in Walter Isaacson's biography and on Lex Fridman's podcast. The April 28 testimony is the first time the account has been delivered under oath.</p>

  <div class="oai-doc">
    <div class="oai-doc-header">openai.com — December 11, 2015 — founding announcement</div>
    <div class="oai-doc-title">Introducing OpenAI</div>
    <div class="oai-doc-mission">"to advance digital intelligence in the way that is most likely to benefit humanity as a whole, unconstrained by a need to generate financial return"</div>
    <div class="oai-doc-meta">
      <div>Form: <span>501(c)(3) Nonprofit</span></div>
      <div>Co-chairs: <span>Sam Altman, Elon Musk</span></div>
      <div>Pledged: <span>$1,000,000,000</span></div>
      <div>Backers: <span>Musk, Altman, Hoffman, Thiel, Livingston, AWS, Infosys</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="mutation">
  <h2><span class="num">02 — The Mutation</span>From 501(c)(3) to ~$850B in Eleven Years</h2>
  <p>The structural arc from the founding post to April 2026 is documented in OpenAI's own filings, in the SEC and AG filings of its partners, and in the diary of one of its co-founders. The diary is now a court exhibit.</p>
  <p>In 2017, two years after the founding post, internal tension over the nonprofit's path reached the point of negotiation. Musk attempted to take over OpenAI and proposed merging it with Tesla; the other founders refused. Greg Brockman's contemporaneous diary entries from September 2017, now entered into evidence, read in part: "This is the only chance we have to get out from Elon... Financially, what will take me to $1B?" A separate Brockman entry, also in evidence, acknowledges: "we weren't honest with him in the end about still wanting to do the for-profit." Judge Gonzalez Rogers cited both entries in her January 2026 ruling on summary judgment.</p>
  <p>In 2018, Musk departed the OpenAI board. In March 2019 — thirteen months after his departure — OpenAI created a capped-profit subsidiary, OpenAI LP, with returns to investors limited to 100x. Microsoft invested $1 billion, roughly half in Azure compute credits. In January 2023, two months after the public release of ChatGPT, Microsoft invested an additional $10 billion, valuing the company at approximately $20 billion. In 2023, Musk founded the rival AI company xAI. In October 2025, OpenAI completed a restructuring as a Public Benefit Corporation, OpenAI Group PBC. The original nonprofit retained a 26 percent stake; Microsoft received a 27 percent stake; employees and other investors held the remaining 47 percent. The conversion was approved by California Attorney General Rob Bonta and by Delaware's attorney general, with binding safety and oversight conditions attached. By the time of trial in April 2026, the company's reported valuation ranged from approximately $850 billion to $1 trillion, with an IPO expected later in the year.</p>
  <p>OpenAI's defense, delivered in opening statements by attorney William Savitt, does not dispute the structural arc. Savitt argued instead that Musk was presented with for-profit restructuring options — pointing to a Shivon Zilis email to Musk's chief of staff, Sam Teller, that laid out two corporate paths: "Roll everything into a B corp" or "OpenAI C Corp and OpenAI non-profit." OpenAI's position is that Musk supported a for-profit structure contingent on his own personal control, and that the breach being alleged is therefore a breach of his control, not the charity's mission.</p>

  <div class="oai-evolution">
